module bindings.java.html_sanitizer_images_url_without_ssrf_guard;

// The OWASP Java HTML Sanitizer's javadoc states what each stock factory in
// `Sanitizers` concedes: `IMAGES` "allows 'img' elements with 'src' allowing
// http, https, and relative URLs". That is a URL-bearing, resource-fetching
// attribute admitted with no attribute value policy at all -- any host the
// markup names survives cleaning. The neighboring `LINKS` factory concedes
// `a href`, which a reader navigates rather than a renderer fetches, so it is
// not this recognition. A sanitizer composition that includes `IMAGES` is
// therefore handing every downstream consumer a set of attacker-chosen URLs,
// and the consumer decides the impact: a browser display makes the client
// fetch them, while a server-side renderer -- the HTML-to-PDF, preview and
// mail-rendering pipelines this pattern feeds -- performs the fetch itself
// from the server's network position, which is server-side request forgery
// through markup the application cleaned. The stock factory cannot be
// parameterised, so the only remedies are dropping it from the composition or
// replacing it with a builder that re-allows the attribute through
// `.matching(policy)`, which is what an application that needs images in
// sanitized markup written for a fetching consumer has to do.
//
// The anchor is the qualified `Sanitizers.IMAGES` attribute read -- the
// library's own public constant, the one token that identifies the conceded
// image-source policy regardless of how the rest of the composition is built.
// The judgement lives in the pack rule; this binding only recognises the
// shape.
//
// Residual, stated rather than hidden: the static-import spelling
// (`import static org.owasp.html.Sanitizers.*;` then `.and(IMAGES)`) lowers
// to a bare identifier with no receiver, and a bare `IMAGES` name is not a
// recognisable fact, so that spelling is missed (recall direction). A custom
// `HtmlPolicyBuilder` chain that allows the attribute without a value policy
// concedes the same URLs but is not recognised, because the builder's
// `.matching(...)` continuation is a sibling call in a field initializer
// where no context tokens exist, so its absence cannot be told from a chain
// that guards the attribute one step later (recall direction, chosen over a
// negation that would fire on the guarded form).
binding owaspSanitizerImagesUrlWithoutSsrfGuard {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "attr"
    and node.path == "Sanitizers.IMAGES"
  emit issue code.SanitizerImageUrlWithoutSsrfGuard at node
}
